introduction:
  bigQuestion: "Why do we need different chips for different jobs?"
  overview: "This HL unit explores <strong>Specialisation</strong> and <strong>Concurrency</strong>. You will move beyond the basic CPU to understand why modern computing relies on <strong>Massive Parallelism (GPUs)</strong> and <strong>Pipelining</strong> to handle complex tasks like AI and 3D rendering. You will also dive into how high-level code is translated into machine code via <strong>Interpreters, Compilers, and Virtual Machines</strong>."
  journey: "You'll begin by comparing the diverse skillset of the <strong>CPU</strong> vs the raw power of the <strong>GPU</strong>. Then you'll optimize execution using <strong>Pipelining</strong> and investigate the abstraction layers of <strong>Bytecode</strong> and <strong>JIT Compilation</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "CPU vs GPU Architecture"
    icon: "fa-microchip"
    content: |
      <p>Different tools for different jobs:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>CPU (Swiss Army Knife):</strong> Few powerful cores, Complex control, Low latency. Good for sequential logic (OS, Apps).</li>
          <li><strong>GPU (Chainsaw):</strong> Thousands of weak cores, High throughput. Good for parallel data (Graphics, AI, Mining).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Pipelining"
    icon: "fa-forward-fast"
    content: |
      <p>How to make a slow processor feel fast. Pipelining overlaps instructions:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Sequential:</strong> Fetch 1 -> Decode 1 -> Execute 1 -> Fetch 2...</li>
          <li><strong>Pipelined:</strong> Fetch 3 | Decode 2 | Execute 1 (All happening at once).</li>
          <li><strong>Implication:</strong> Increases <strong>Throughput</strong> (tasks per second), not individual speed.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Translation: Compilers vs Interpreters"
    icon: "fa-language"
    content: |
      <p>Computers only speak binary. How do we get there?</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Compiler:</strong> Translate Everything at once -> Run later. (Fast execution, Slow dev).</li>
          <li><strong>Interpreter:</strong> Translate Line-by-Line -> Run immediately. (Slow execution, Fast dev).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Virtual Machines & JIT"
    icon: "fa-boxes-packing"
    content: |
      <p>The best of both worlds? Java and Python use <strong>Bytecode</strong>.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Virtual Machine:</strong> Software CPU that runs Bytecode anywhere (Portability).</li>
          <li><strong>JIT (Just-In-Time):</strong> The VM compiles hot code to machine code <em>while running</em> for speed.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Parallelism"
    definition: "Doing multiple things at exactly the same time (e.g., GPU cores working on different pixels)."
  - term: "Concurrency"
    definition: "Dealing with multiple things at once (e.g., Pipelining features of a single core)."
  - term: "Throughput"
    definition: "The amount of work performed by a system in a given time."
  - term: "Latency"
    definition: "The time it takes to process a single instruction from start to finish."
  - term: "Pipelining"
    definition: "A technique where multiple instructions are overlapped in execution."
  - term: "Bytecode"
    definition: "Intermediate code (like a half-way translation) that usually runs on a Virtual Machine."
  - term: "Virtual Machine (VM)"
    definition: "Software that emulates hardware, allowing code to run on any physical machine."
  - term: "JIT Compilation"
    definition: "Just-In-Time. Compiling bytecode into native machine code at runtime to improve performance."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Pipelining makes instructions faster"
    correction: "No! Each instruction takes the same time. But you finish MORE instructions per second because you overlap them."
  - mistake: "GPU is better than CPU"
    correction: "Try running Windows on a GPU. It would crash. GPUs are terrible at complex logic/branching; they only excel at repetitive math."
  - mistake: "Compiler = Better"
    correction: "Not always. Interpreters allow cross-platform portability and easier debugging. It's a trade-off."

